<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="hydroxylated mugineic acid phytosiderophore biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5925" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group, and, enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Graminaceous plants use the chelating method for iron absorption (also referred to as strategy II Fe-uptake). These plants secrete Fe-chelating siderophores of the |FRAME: CPD-7231| type into the rhizopshere. seven of these compounds, which have six coordination sites (3 -COOH, 2 -NH and 1 -OH) that bind Fe(III), have been identified. Extensive biochemical and physiological studies have identified the pathway and the corresponding genes |CITS: [8061321]|. Variations in the amounts of phytosiderophore secreted by different species lead to differences in tolerance to iron defficiency |CITS: [11346963]|. |FRAME: TAX-4513|, which is the most resistant to Fe-deficieny, secretes very high amounts of |FRAME: CPD-7231|-type siderophores, including the hydroxylated species |FRAME: CPD-7291| and |FRAME: CPD-7289| |CITS: [9952442]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="nilo-poyanco" />
    <Author name="O-27" />
